## Hard rules

- **Dev-only at runtime.** Both toggles live in `features/dev/controls/submenus/NavigationSubmenu.tsx` ("Use shortcuts" + "Prefer elevators"). CU never sees them. Default states are OFF/OFF, so production users get the same NavMesh routing as before — never let shortcut behaviour leak into CU without an explicit design pass + WCAG review (elevator metaphor needs labelling, alternative routes for users who can't take an elevator IRL, etc.). The CU `PreferencesGrids.tsx` "Elevators" radio currently does **not** drive `shortcutsPreferToggle` — wiring that link is a future task explicitly held back so production stays on direct walking.
- **Guided routes only.** Shortcuts are gated by `SHORTCUT_ROUTE_IDS` in `navmesh_route_service/constants.py` — currently `{seat_nav, exit_nav, poi_nav, quiet_zone_nav}`. The point-and-click `player` route never uses shortcuts, even with the toggle on; map clicks must remain literal walking.
- **One hop per trip.** `ShortcutRouter.find_best_plan()` returns single-hop plans only. Multi-hop chains (e.g. elevator + bus + elevator) are YAGNI for the stadium and would invalidate the post-teleport recompose model. Don't add multi-hop without rewriting the resume flow.
- **No direct teleport calls from frontend.** The Kit-side `ShortcutTraversalService` is the *only* place that performs the elevator teleport. The frontend's role is the fade animation handshake (`shortcutTraversalBegin` → `shortcutTraversalProceed`) — never invoke `teleport_to_coordinates` from web for shortcut hops.
- **`prefer_shortcuts` never bypasses validity.** Three layers of filtering decide whether a hop is even considered:
  - **Section gate (seat routes).** When `corridor_section` is set on the compose call (i.e. it's a `seat_nav` route), the router scans every loaded shortcut group and collects those whose `servesSections` (declared in `shortcuts.json`, parsed into `ShortcutGroup.serves_sections`) contains the section letter. If the resulting set is **empty** — either because the section is lower-bowl and no elevator declares it, or because it's an upper section without a nearby lift yet (E, F, H today) — `find_best_plan` returns `None` immediately. The OP/RS lifts on the opposite side of the building would force a half-ring walk-out leg for E/F/H, so until an `elevator_3` lands on the GH/DEF side, walking the staircase is the only sensible option.
  - **Per-hop group gate (seat routes).** For sections that pass, only candidate hops whose `group_id` is in the section's allowed set survive — every other group is skipped before the spatial pre-filter. Today `elevator_2.servesSections = ["P","S"]`, so P/S routes consider only `elevator_2`. Non-seat routes (POIs / ad-hoc positions) skip this filter and consider every group.
  - **Per-hop vertical filters.** Always: basement-dip / wrong-way / same-band-dip in `shortcut_router.vertical_filters.skip_hop_exit_vertical_backtrack`. Prefer-mode-only **and only for non-seat routes**: the exit must additionally land within `ELEVATOR_EXIT_TO_DEST_MAX_CM` in `shortcut_router/constants.py` (≈ 100 cm) of the destination Y (POIs / generic positions). Seat routes skip the landing-Y check because the seat Y is the bowl tier, not the concourse the seat is entered from — the section gate above already established a useful lift exists.

  Concretely:
  - `A-15-1` (section A) → section gate rejects (no group lists "A") → walk the bowl stairs.
  - `P-35-5` (section P) → section gate passes (`elevator_2` lists "P") → group gate restricts candidates to `elevator_2` → vertical-valid hop wins (in strict if shorter, in prefer always). `elevator_1` is **never** considered for P even if its spatial lower bound ranks first.
  - `H-12-3` / `E-2-1` / `F-…` → section gate rejects (no group lists "H"/"E"/"F"). When an `elevator_3` is added on the GH/DEF side, just set its `"servesSections": ["E","F","H"]` in `shortcuts.json` — no Python change.
  - `quiet_zone_03` ("Quiet area 01" at y=1942, no section) → no section gate → all groups considered → close-Y check passes (`elevator_1.floor_0` at y=1922 is 20 cm away) → lift wins in prefer mode.
  - Cross-building POIs (`quiet_zone_01` / `_02` near other venues) → strict mode loses on cost; prefer mode usually loses because the NavMesh leg from any lift exit to the distant POI fails or is enormous.

  Adding a new upper section means: (a) extend `waypoint_router.UPPER_SECTION_BRANCH` with the section's branch chain, and (b) add the section letter to the relevant group's `"servesSections"` in `shortcuts.json`. No constants or dicts to edit under `shortcut_router/` (tuning lives in `shortcut_router/constants.py` if you change global thresholds).

