## Runtime architecture

```
        shortcutsToggle (dev menu)        shortcutsPreferToggle (dev menu)
                  │                                       │
                  ▼                                       ▼
    set_shortcuts_active(True)              set_prefer_shortcuts(True)
                  │                                       │
                  └─── cfg.use_shortcuts ─┬───── cfg.prefer_shortcuts ───┘
                                          ▼
            RouteComposer.compose(..., enable_shortcuts=True,
                                       prefer_shortcuts=?)
                                          │
                       ┌──────────────────┴──────────────────┐
                       ▼                                     ▼
            _try_shortcut_route(start, end)         fall through to NavMesh / OSM
                       │
                       ▼
            ShortcutRouter.find_best_plan(start, end, walk_len,
                                          prefer_shortcut=?)
                       │
            ┌──────────┴──────────────────────┐
            │ same-tier (|dy| < min_gap/2) ?  │  min_gap = smallest Y step
            │ (skipped when prefer_shortcuts) │   between shortcut nodes
            └──────────┬──────────────────────┘
              skip ────┘                no
                                              │
                                              ▼
                       prefer_shortcut == True ?
                ┌──────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┐
                ▼                                                     ▼
      best_cost = inf                                         direct_cm = walk_len(s,e)
      (any valid hop wins)                                    best_cost = direct_cm
                                                                                │
                                              ┌─────────────────────────────────┘
                                              ▼
                              vertical filters (basement-dip / wrong-way / same-band)
                                              │
                                              ▼
                              find_best_plan returns ShortcutPlan or None
                                              │
                                              ▼
                ComposedRoute([navmesh leg, shortcut leg, navmesh leg])
                                              │
                                              ▼
                segment_speeds + segment_classes → frontend dispatch
                                              │
                                              ▼
                  PointClickAutoMover walks leg 1
                                              │
                                              ▼
                  emits playerPathClassChange (next="shortcut")
                                              │
                                              ▼
                  ShortcutTraversalService._on_path_class_change
                       │
                       ▼
              shortcutTraversalBegin ───────────────► frontend fade-out
                                                              │
              shortcutTraversalProceed ◄───────────────────────┘ (onFadeOutComplete)
                       │
                       ▼
              TeleportService.teleport_to_coordinates(exit_pos)
                       │
                       ▼
              viewTransitionReady ──────────────────► frontend fade-in
                       │
                       ▼
              shortcutTraversalComplete + recompose route from new player position
```

When `_try_shortcut_route` wins, **`compose()` still short-circuits** before `_same_navmesh`, but **outer walk legs** use corridor helpers tuned for hops:

- **Walk to the lift** — **`plan_route_to_position`** whenever any corridor mode is on, so a seat **section** never pulls the full upper-bowl branch (e.g. OP→…→P) toward a foyer-level door.
- **Walk from the lift (seat_nav with `corridor_section`)** — **`plan_route(player, seat, section, floor_hint_y=exit_y, branch_join_pos=exit_xyz)`** (same function and section letter as the initial `seatNavigate` corridor plan). Effects in `waypoint_router.py`:
  - **`floor_hint_y`** — drops branch prims whose **Y** is below `floor_hint_y - _DEFAULT_BRANCH_FLOOR_TOL_CM` (450 cm) so landings below the exit floor are not revisited.
  - **`branch_join_pos`** — after that trim, the branch **suffix** starts at the chain waypoint **closest in 3D** to the exit, considering only **staircase + intermediates** (not the section anchor), **unless** the exit is within **`_JOIN_AT_SECTION_ANCHOR_MAX_CM` (700 cm)** of the anchor prim — then the suffix is **anchor-only** (already at corridor end). Stops false “nearest is P” joins that skipped OP_2/OP_3.
  - **Skip main ring** — when the first kept branch prim is **not** the staircase head (e.g. rejoin at `nav_waypoint_OP_2`), the **ring segment** is omitted; entrance-only prepend still applies if the player is outside.

**`ShortcutRouter.find_best_plan`** ranks hop candidates with a **Y-alignment** term and a higher pair-evaluation cap so the exit floor matches destination height better than XZ-only ordering.  It also **drops** the entire pre-pass / individual hops on four different grounds:

* **Section gate (always, both modes).** If `corridor_section` is set, the router collects every loaded group whose `serves_sections` contains the section letter. Empty result → `find_best_plan` returns `None` immediately. With today's `shortcuts.json`, P and S resolve to `{elevator_2}`; A/B/C/D/E/F/G/H/I/J/… resolve to `{}` and walk.
* **Per-hop group gate (seat routes, always).** For sections that pass the gate, candidate hops whose `group_id` isn't in the section's allowed set are skipped before the spatial pre-filter. This stops `elevator_1` (A-side) from ever ranking first for an OP/RS-side P seat just because some XZ lower bound looks small.
* **Per-hop vertical filters (always).** Seat above player but exit well **below** the start (basement then climb back), the symmetric case, or — when ``|ΔY|`` start→end **&lt; 900 cm** — an exit more than **~280 cm** below **both** endpoints (same-band basement dip).
* **Per-hop landing-Y check (prefer mode + non-seat routes only).** ``|exit_y − end_y| ≤ ELEVATOR_EXIT_TO_DEST_MAX_CM`` (≈ 100 cm, `shortcut_router/constants.py`). For POIs / generic positions where the destination Y is the floor it sits on (`quiet_zone_03` at y=1942 vs `floor_0` at y=1922 = 20 cm apart → accepted). Disabled for seat routes because the seat Y is the bowl tier, not the concourse Y — the section gate already filtered out the seats that would never use a lift.

**Composer** short-circuits the shortcut pre-pass when ``|ΔY| < max(min_gap/2, 750 cm)`` so nearby seats (e.g. same-tier A row) never enter lift scoring — but only after **probing direct walking** (one extra `_navmesh_leg(start, end, ctx)` call) so the optimisation is honoured only when walking actually succeeds. If the probe returns ``None`` — classic case is a wheelchair user navigating to the lower-restaurant quiet zone (~286 cm below the concourse, well under the tier_cut, but stairs blocked on the wheelchair NavMesh) — the skip is bypassed and `find_best_plan` runs with `direct_cm=None`, so any vertically-valid lift hop wins. The probe is bypassed entirely when `prefer_shortcuts` is on (the user explicitly asked for "lift if valid"). The section gate / landing-Y rule then decides whether the lift is genuinely useful.

This dual mechanism is what lets `Wheelchair + Use shortcuts ON + Prefer OFF` reach destinations behind stairs without the user having to also flip "Prefer elevators". When walking succeeds the strict-shortest comparison runs as normal (so a wheelchair user on the ground floor still walks to a same-floor seat and doesn't get sent through a lift); when walking fails the lift becomes the route — same behaviour the user would get by manually toggling prefer on, just triggered automatically by an unreachable walk instead of by a UI flip.

**Cost baseline:** `SHORTCUT_SLACK_FRACTION = 0.0` in `shortcut_router/constants.py` — there is no slack window any more. The decision is binary:

- `prefer_shortcut == False` → `best_cost = direct_cm` → a hop must be **strictly cheaper** than the direct NavMesh walk to win.
- `prefer_shortcut == True` → `direct_cm` is set to `None` and `best_cost` starts at infinity → **any** hop that survives the vertical filters wins, regardless of how it compares to walking. If no hop is valid (e.g. seat A-15-1 from the basement plaza with no lift opening on that band), the composer falls back to direct NavMesh walking — a route is always returned when one exists.

This is what makes wheelchair routing safe: when an upper-deck quiet zone is unreachable on the wheelchair NavMesh because of stairs, toggling "Use shortcuts" + "Prefer elevators" ON still picks the lift hop instead of returning "no route" — provided a valid lift exists on both ends.

