## Dev testing the toggles

Both toggles live under the dev sidebar → **Navigation → Use shortcuts / Prefer elevators**. The "Prefer elevators" button is locked off whenever "Use shortcuts" is off — there is no valid state where prefer is ON but shortcuts are OFF.

Behaviour matrix (for the four guided routes in `SHORTCUT_ROUTE_IDS`):

| Use shortcuts | Prefer elevators | Wheelchair mode | Effect |
|---|---|---|---|
| OFF | (locked off) | any | Strictly NavMesh walking. The codepath is **identical to master** — `cfg.use_shortcuts == False` skips the shortcut pre-pass entirely. Use this as your regression baseline. |
| ON | OFF | OFF | "Strict shortest". `find_best_plan` picks a hop only if `shortcut_total < direct_cm`. If the lift is longer (e.g. for a same-section seat), the route stays on stairs. |
| ON | OFF | ON | Same strict-shortest rule against the **wheelchair** NavMesh. **When walking is unreachable** (e.g. lower-restaurant quiet zone via stairs), the composer's same-tier walking probe fails and `find_best_plan` runs against `direct_cm = None`, so any vertically-valid lift hop wins automatically — no need to also flip "Prefer elevators". The discovery layer mirrors this via `compute_routes_to_exits`'s shortcut-aware fallback, so the destination shows up in POI / quiet-zone panels with `viaShortcut: true`. |
| ON | ON | OFF | "Always lift if valid". Vertical filters still apply. Useful for reproducing CU-equivalent "Elevators preferred" behaviour and stress-testing the post-hop corridor rebuild. |
| ON | ON | ON | Same as above for the wheelchair NavMesh. Forces the lift even when walking would have been reachable on the wheelchair mesh — used to stress-test the post-hop corridor rebuild without relying on the unreachable-walking auto-promotion. |

Suggested smoke tests after touching anything in this stack:

1. **Default off, default off.** Trigger seat-nav to A-15-1 from main entrance — the route must be byte-identical to a build off `master`. Watch the kit log for the **absence** of any `[shortcut_router]` line.
2. **Use shortcuts ON, prefer OFF, walking mode.** Same A-15-1 trip should still walk the stairs (lift is longer); log shows `no shortcut beats direct` or, if the same-tier skip fires, no log at all. Now trigger a destination on a different floor where the lift is genuinely cheaper — log shows `shortcut wins … (strict_beat)` and the player rides.
3. **Use shortcuts ON, prefer ON, walking mode.** Test four distinct destination categories — each one exercises a different gate:
   * **Lower-bowl seat** (e.g. `A-15-1`, any seat in B/C/D/G/I/J/…) — **section gate** rejects (no group lists "A" in its `servesSections`). Route walks the stairs. No `[shortcut_router]` log line.
   * **Upper-bowl seat with no elevator yet** (e.g. any seat in `E`, `F`, `H`) — **section gate** rejects (no group lists "E"/"F"/"H"). Route walks the staircase via the GH/DEF branch. No `[shortcut_router]` log line. When `elevator_3` lands on that side, set its `"servesSections": ["E","F","H"]` in `shortcuts.json` and re-run — no Python edit needed.
   * **Upper-bowl seat with a mapped elevator** (e.g. `P-35-5`, any seat in `P` or `S`) — section gate passes (`elevator_2` lists those), **per-hop group gate** restricts candidates to `elevator_2`. Log shows `shortcut wins … group=elevator_2 … (prefer)`. Confirm the route never picks `elevator_1` even though it ranks higher on raw distance: with both lifts loaded, force a verbose run and verify `elevator_1` candidates are skipped before the spatial pre-filter.
   * **Tier-aligned POI** (e.g. `quiet_zone_03` / "Quiet area 01" at y=1942 — 20 cm above `floor_0`, or any restroom on a lift landing) — no section gate (POIs skip it), landing-Y check passes, lift wins in prefer. Log shows `shortcut wins … (prefer)`.
4. **Use shortcuts ON, prefer OFF, wheelchair mode, click the lower-restaurant quiet zone.** This is the regression test for the same-tier-skip walking probe AND the discovery fallback in one shot. The destination is only ~286 cm below the player on the concourse (well under the 750 cm tier_cut), so the same-tier skip would normally bail before reaching `find_best_plan`; and the destination is behind stairs on the wheelchair NavMesh, so the legacy `compute_route_measure` reports `success=False` and the panel would normally hide the entry.
   * **Discovery side** — open the quiet-zones panel. The "Quiet area 01" entry must appear with a normal distance/time figure and `viaShortcut: true`. Toggle "Use shortcuts" OFF and the same destination must disappear again. Logs to look for: a single `[shortcut_router] shortcut wins: group=elevator_1 ...` line per visible entry on the panel-open compose pass.
   * **Navigation side** — click "Get directions" / "Start navigation" on that entry. The composer detects walking is unreachable (probe returns `None`), bypasses the same-tier skip, and `find_best_plan` rescues the route with `elevator_1`. The card must stay open (no `navmeshRouteError` flash), the route is drawn, the player rides the lift on auto-move with the same instant-jump feel as walking + prefer ON.
5. **Use shortcuts ON, prefer ON, wheelchair mode.** Same as test #4 but with prefer ON — verifies the explicit-prefer path also works on the wheelchair mesh and produces the same lift route. Useful to confirm that the prefer toggle remains independently functional in wheelchair mode (i.e. the auto-promotion in test #4 hasn't shadowed it).
6. **Toggle prefer ON mid-trip.** While walking a strict-shortest stair route, flip "Prefer elevators" — `set_prefer_shortcuts` calls `recalc_now()` on the active route and the polyline should redraw via the lift on the next compose. Then turn it back off — the route should snap back to walking.

When filing a bug, include the `[shortcut_router]` line and the surrounding `route accepted` line — those two pin down both the routing decision and the drawn polyline length.

